[[string]]
id = "help.overview.a1"
text = "Force and field are what you need for a trajectory: the direction of the motion, the acceleration, where the charge will be in a second. Energy and potential answer “what happens at the end” without solving the motion: how fast the charge will arrive, whether it can reach a point, whether it will escape to infinity."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.a2"
text = "A magnetic field appears, and the force becomes \\(\\ F = q(E + v \\times B)\\). The magnetic part is perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work: the speed still changes only because of the electric field. Topics 15–19 are that part of the program. What is still left out is the circulating electric field a changing magnetic field would create. For that field the work would depend on the path, and it would no longer be only the slope of a potential."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.answer"
text = "The speed, yes, from conservation of energy: the charge times the potential difference between A and B equals the gain in kinetic energy. The time, no: that needs the trajectory, that is, the field along the whole path, not only the values at two points."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 21"

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.legend1"
text = "the field — a vector, force per unit charge (\\(\\mathrm{{N}}/\\mathrm{{C}} = \\mathrm{{V}}/\\mathrm{{m}}\\)). A property of a point."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.legend2"
text = "the potential — a scalar, energy per unit charge (\\(\\mathrm{{V}} = \\mathrm{{J}}/\\mathrm{{C}}\\)). A property of a point."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.legend3"
text = "the force — a vector (N). A property of one particular charge at a point."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.legend4"
text = "potential energy — a scalar (J). A property of a configuration of charges."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.note"
text = "Three main ideas. \\(\\ E\\) says what will happen to a charge at a point (the force per unit charge it will feel). \\(\\ \\varphi\\) describes the electric state of a point through energy per unit charge. \\(\\ U\\) is the energy state of one particular configuration. Differences of \\(\\ \\varphi\\) and of \\(\\ U\\) are what carry physical meaning, not the values themselves."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.p1"
text = "Basic electrostatics is one chain. Source charges create a field \\(\\ E\\). The field sets the force on any charge, \\(\\ F = qE\\). The same field can be described by a potential \\(\\ \\varphi\\), because \\(\\ E = -\\nabla\\varphi\\). The potential turns the description into the language of energy: \\(\\ U = q\\varphi\\). A change in energy is the work of the field, \\(\\ \\Delta U = -W\\), and that work becomes the kinetic energy of the motion."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.p2"
text = "So \\(\\ E\\), \\(\\ \\varphi\\), and \\(\\ U\\) are not three separate theories. They are three ways to describe the same system: through force per unit charge, through energy per unit charge, and through the energy of one particular configuration. You pick the one that fits the question."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.p3"
text = "Each link in the chain has its own layer. The vector grid and the probe are the field \\(\\ E\\) (F3, F5). The background with equipotentials is \\(\\ \\varphi\\) (F1). Field lines and flow are the structure of the field (F2, F4). The energy panel is \\(\\ U\\), \\(\\ K\\), and the total \\(\\ K + U\\). Trails are the motion that the energy turns into (F6). Magnetic marks (F7) show \\(\\ B_z\\), and the Lorentz force (M) lets that field turn the motion (topics 15–19)."

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.predict"
text = "You know only \\(\\ \\varphi\\) at points A and B. Can you find the speed with which a charge released at A arrives at B? And the time of flight?"

[[string]]
id = "help.overview.q1"
text = "When is it easier to think in forces, and when in energy?"

