[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.a1"
text = "Because bringing them in from infinity means pushing against repulsion the whole way. An outside force does positive work, and that work is stored as the energy of the configuration. A system you release pays it back by flying apart."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.a2"
text = "Because, as you bring them in from infinity, the field itself does positive work — the energy is given away, not stored. The configuration ends up below the level “infinitely far apart”, that is, below zero. Returning the charges to infinity means giving that energy back."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.a3"
text = "\\(U = k\\frac{{Qq}}{{r}}\\) with \\(\\ Qq < 0\\) is a negative number whose magnitude is inversely proportional to the distance. Closer means a larger magnitude, so \\(\\ U\\) is more negative (for example \\(\\ -100\\) instead of \\(\\ -50\\)). Farther means a smaller magnitude, so \\(\\ U\\) is closer to zero: \\(\\ -50\\), \\(\\ -10\\), 0. “Rises” here means “becomes less negative”."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.a4"
text = "Because \\(\\ U\\) is the energy of the pair’s interaction, not of each charge separately. If you walk through every charge and add \\(\\ q\\varphi\\) from the others, each pair is counted twice — and then you have to divide by 2."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.answer1"
text = "The pair’s energy \\(\\ k\\frac{{1\\cdot 1}}{{2}} = 0.5\\) becomes kinetic, shared equally: each charge has kinetic energy \\(\\ 0.25\\), so the speed is \\(\\ v \\approx 0.71\\). Check the panel: \\(\\ K\\) rises nearly to 0.5, \\(\\ U\\) falls nearly to 0, and the sum \\(\\ K + U\\) hardly changes. With the usual softening \\(\\ \\varepsilon = 0.25\\) the start has \\(\\ U \\approx 0.496\\), so \\(\\ K\\) ends near 0.496 rather than exactly 0.5."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.answer2"
text = "The ones that fly closest to the axis through the nucleus: they approach more closely, where the repulsion is stronger. The most central one turns almost all the way back."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 11"

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.legend1"
text = "the energy of a pair at distance \\(\\ r\\). The zero is at infinite distance."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.legend2"
text = "the product of the charges. Its sign sets the sign of \\(\\ U\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.legend3"
text = "the sum over every pair of charges, each pair once."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.legend4"
text = "the distance between charges i and j."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.legend5"
text = "the closest distance a charge reaches when it flies in from far away, straight at a fixed charge of the same sign, and momentarily stops."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.note"
text = "The last formula is Rutherford’s experiment (1911). Alpha particles were fired at gold foil, and some bounced back. Stopping a fast particle like that takes a very small distance of closest approach — hence the conclusion that the positive charge of an atom is gathered into a tiny nucleus."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.p1"
text = "Two charges a distance \\(\\ r\\) apart share a potential energy \\(\\ U = k\\frac{{Qq}}{{r}}\\) — if the zero is chosen at an infinite separation. This is the energy of their configuration: it equals the work it would take to bring them in from infinity to the distance \\(\\ r\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.p2"
text = "Like charges: \\(\\ U > 0\\). Bringing them together takes work against the repulsion, and that work is stored. Let them go and they fly apart, \\(\\ U\\) falls to zero, and the stored energy becomes kinetic."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.p3"
text = "Opposite charges: \\(\\ U < 0\\). The field itself draws them together and does positive work, so as they approach, \\(\\ U\\) becomes more and more negative and the kinetic energy grows. Separating them to infinity takes energy you have to add: \\(\\ U\\) rises toward zero — the number increases, even though its magnitude shrinks."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.p4"
text = "For several charges the energy is the sum over every pair, each pair once. That is the quantity shown on the panel as “Potential \\(\\ U\\)”."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.p5"
text = "The “Potential \\(\\ U\\)” row on the panel is exactly the sum over pairs. Because of softening (the appendix), the program uses a distance that is slightly “softened” near zero, so the values are close to the formula but stay finite even in a collision."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.p6"
text = "In “Rutherford” the nucleus has charge 4 and the projectiles have charge \\(\\ 0.25\\) at speed 2, so a head-on \\(\\ r_{{\\min}} = 0.5\\). The balls in the program already touch at about 0.78, so the central projectile bounces off the nucleus ball. The ones that pass to the side draw the clean scattering hyperbolas."

