[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.a1"
text = "The update \\(\\ x \\leftarrow x + v\\,\\Delta t\\) is the explicit Euler method: it systematically adds energy on every step. On an orbit the charge slowly spirals outward, even though the physical orbit is steady. Leapfrog builds the step so that it is symmetric in time, and it does not have that systematic error."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.a2"
text = "With no collisions and no speed cap, to a relative error below 0.1% over 20,000 steps of a circular orbit. An automatic test checks that. The total energy on the panel changes noticeably only because of collisions, the speed cap, or dragging with the mouse."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.answer"
text = "Close encounters weaken: the charges behave like large blurred balls, forces at short range are smaller, and clusters become looser. Distant interactions hardly change."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.kicker"
text = "APPENDIX"

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p1"
text = "The charges are point particles in the plane of the screen, but they follow the \\(\\ \\sim \\frac{{1}}{{r^2}}\\) law of the real three-dimensional world: this is a slice of a 3D scene, not “two-dimensional physics”. Every vector in the code is three-dimensional with \\(\\ z = 0\\), so the forces and the motion stay in the plane. The field is instantaneous: changes spread with no delay. Moving charges also make a magnetic field perpendicular to the screen; the Lorentz force (M) is what lets it act on them (topics 15–19)."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p2"
text = "The program advances the motion in fixed steps of \\(\\ \\frac{{1}}{{60}}\\) of a second (at a higher time scale, in several smaller steps). The method is leapfrog, or velocity Verlet: half a step of velocity, a full step of position, then another half step of velocity with the new force. Unlike the simplest \\(\\ x \\leftarrow x + v\\,\\Delta t\\), it is symplectic: the energy does not drift away, it only jitters a little, so orbits do not spiral out or fall in over time. That is the electric part of the step."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p3"
text = "For point charges the force is unbounded as \\(\\ r \\to 0\\), and a close flyby would fling charges off at huge speeds. So the distance in every formula is “softened” by \\(\\ \\varepsilon\\) (the slider on the panel): a charge behaves like a blurred ball of size about \\(\\ \\varepsilon\\). The softening is the same for the electric field, the magnetic field, the potential, and the energy, so \\(\\ E = -\\nabla\\varphi\\) and energy conservation hold exactly. Far from a charge (a distance much larger than \\(\\ \\varepsilon\\)) there is no difference."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p4"
text = "Charges are balls of radius \\(\\ 0.2 + 0.15\\sqrt{{|q|}}\\). When the balls overlap, they are pushed apart, and the relative velocity reverses and is multiplied by a coefficient of restitution of 0.8: momentum is conserved, and some kinetic energy disappears (in reality, as heat). The K key turns collisions off. Speed is capped at 30, and also at \\(\\ 0.9c\\) (the speed-of-light slider, default 20), so no flyby can throw a charge to infinity and the charges stay slower than light. The panel shows the fastest \\(\\ v/c\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p5"
text = "Everything is in arbitrary units: \\(\\ k = 1\\) (the slider), charge mass 1, distances in grid units, time in simulation seconds. That is only a choice of scale. The formulas are the same as in SI; only the numbers change. To move to SI, replace \\(\\ k\\) with \\(\\ \\frac{{1}}{{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}} \\approx 8.99\\cdot 10^{{9}}\\) in SI units (topic 2), charges with coulombs, and distances with meters. The magnetic constant is not a separate slider: \\(\\ \\frac{{\\mu_0}}{{4\\pi}} = \\frac{{k}}{{c^2}}\\), with \\(\\ c\\) the speed of light (topic 15)."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p6"
text = "The potential background is computed by the graphics card for every pixel. Field lines are traced with a fourth-order Runge–Kutta method and rebuilt only when the charges or the camera change. Flow particles and the vector grid read the same field. Softening and the \\(\\ \\sim \\frac{{1}}{{r^2}}\\) law are the same in every layer and in the physics of the motion."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p7"
text = "An atom does not look like the “Orbit” scene. An electron is described by a wave function, not a trajectory, and a classical charge on an orbit would radiate and fall into the nucleus. An electron does not fall into a proton because of the uncertainty relation, not because their fields “overlap” — fields always overlap and add. The Pauli principle explains something else: why many electrons, and a piece of matter, do not collapse into one point. Protons and neutrons are made of quarks. A particle and an antiparticle can annihilate. Softening and collisions in the program are deliberate stand-ins for these effects, not a model of them (topics 1–2)."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.p8"
text = "The program also leaves out: the circulating electric field of a changing magnetic field (Faraday’s law), the finite speed at which the field spreads, radiation from accelerating charges, conductors and dielectrics, and the fact that real field lines spread in three dimensions (topic 6). The magnetic field of moving charges is included (topics 15–19)."

[[string]]
id = "help.simulation.predict"
