[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.a1"
text = "They do affect one another — by forces — and if the charges are free they will shove each other around. But for a given arrangement, the field of each charge at a point is the same as if that charge were alone: the presence of the others does not distort it. That is an experimental fact, built into the linearity of Maxwell’s equations. It fails only in extraordinarily strong fields, where quantum electrodynamics takes over."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.a2"
text = "Because the contributions almost completely cancel. The total charge of a quadrupole is zero, so far away there is no “leading” piece that falls as \\(\\ \\sim \\frac{{1}}{{r^2}}\\), and no dipole piece is left either. What remains falls as \\(\\ \\sim \\frac{{1}}{{r^4}}\\) — much faster than the field of a single charge."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.answer1"
text = "Zero. Each pair of opposite corners holds charges of the same sign at the same distance from the center, so their fields at the center are equal and opposite. Check it with the probe (F5): the arrow disappears at the center."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.answer2"
text = "To the right, from the plus toward the minus, and twice as strong. The field from \\(\\ +1\\) points away from it (to the right), the field toward \\(\\ -1\\) points toward it (also to the right), so the contributions add."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 4"

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.legend1"
text = "the field charge i would create if it were alone."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.legend2"
text = "charge i, with its sign. A negative charge reverses its contribution."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.legend3"
text = "the vector from charge i to the point where the field is wanted. Its length is the distance from the charge to the point."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.legend4"
text = "the components of the total field. Each one is an ordinary sum of numbers."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.note"
text = "Potential adds even more simply — as ordinary numbers, with no directions (topic 7). Complicated fields are often easier to find through the potential."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.p1"
text = "The field of several charges equals the vector sum of the fields each charge would create on its own. The field of one charge does not depend on whether others are nearby: at a given arrangement, each charge contributes its own piece, and the pieces simply add. The same is true of forces."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.p2"
text = "The sum has to be a vector sum — directions included. The field of two charges can therefore be weaker than the field of either one, and it can even be zero: the contributions can cancel. In practice it is convenient to add the vectors component by component: all the \\(\\ x\\) pieces together, all the \\(\\ y\\) pieces together."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.p3"
text = "Superposition is not obvious. It is a property of nature: the equations of electromagnetism are linear. That is what lets you take any complicated system of charges apart into simple points and compute the field as a sum."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.p4"
text = "The program computes the field exactly this way: at each point it walks through every charge and adds the contributions. That is how the vector grid (F3), the field lines (F2), the probe reading (F5), and the forces between charges are all obtained."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.predict1"
text = "In the “Quadrupole” scene four charges sit at the corners of a square, with alternating signs. What is the field at the center of the square?"

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.predict2"
