# --- generated: reference extras (begin) ---

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.kicker"
text = "DEEP DIVE"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.title"
text = "Gauss’s law: where it comes from"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p1"
text = "Topic 6 stated Gauss’s law. Here it is built step by step from Coulomb’s law: first for a sphere around one charge, then for any surface, then for many charges. Every step answers one of the questions that usually stay unclear."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.h2"
text = "STEP 1 — THE FLUX THROUGH ONE PATCH"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p3"
text = "Take a tiny patch of a surface, so small that the field on it is the same everywhere. Its area is \\(\\ dA\\). The vector \\(\\ d\\vec{{A}}\\) has that length and points along the outward unit normal \\(\\ n\\), perpendicular to the patch. The flux through the patch is the dot product of the field with this vector."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.fig4"
text = "The patch contributes E·dA·cos θ: the whole of E·dA when the field is along the normal, nothing when it slides along the patch, and a negative amount when it points inward."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p5"
text = "Only the part of the field along the normal “goes through” the patch. A field that skims along the surface does not pass through it, and that is what the cosine says."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.h6"
text = "STEP 2 — A SPHERE AROUND A POINT CHARGE"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p7"
text = "Put a point charge \\(\\ Q\\) at the centre of a sphere of radius \\(\\ r\\). Symmetry gives three facts. Every point of the sphere is at the same distance from the charge, so \\(\\ E\\) is the same everywhere on it. The field is radial. And the area vector of the sphere is radial too. For a positive charge the angle is \\(\\ \\theta = 0^\\circ\\) on every patch."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.fig8"
text = "A point charge at the centre of a sphere: the field E and the area vector dA point the same way everywhere on the sphere, and E has the same value."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p9"
text = "Two things allow us to take \\(\\ E\\) out of the integral: its value is the same on the whole surface, and the cosine is 1. If the value were constant but the angle changed, \\(\\ \\vec E \\cdot d\\vec A\\) could not simply be replaced by \\(\\ E\\,dA\\). The remaining integral \\(\\ \\oint dA\\) just adds the areas of all patches: it is the area of the sphere, \\(\\ 4\\pi r^2\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.h10"
text = "STEP 3 — THE RADIUS CANCELS"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p11"
text = "Now insert the field of a point charge from Coulomb’s law. The radius disappears from the answer: the flux through a sphere around the charge is the same for every radius."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.fig12"
text = "At 2r the field is four times weaker, but the sphere is four times larger, so E·S is the same. The same eight lines cross both spheres: flux counts lines."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p13"
text = "This is the deep reason for the exact \\(\\ 1/r^2\\) in Coulomb’s law. With any other exponent the radius would not cancel, and the flux would depend on the size of the sphere."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.h14"
text = "STEP 4 — ANY SHAPE, NOT ONLY A SPHERE"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p15"
text = "For a lumpy surface the field and the angle change from patch to patch, and nothing can be pulled out of the integral. The way out is the solid angle \\(\\ d\\Omega\\): the opening of the narrow cone that goes from the charge through the patch. A patch at distance \\(\\ r\\) whose normal is tilted by \\(\\ \\theta\\) from the line to the charge cuts out the solid angle"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.fig16"
text = "Three patches in one cone: a nearer one, a farther one that is four times larger, and a tilted one still farther. Their areas differ, but the product E·dA·cos θ is the same: it depends only on the cone."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p17"
text = "A farther patch is larger (its area grows as \\(\\ r^2\\)) but the field there is weaker (as \\(\\ 1/r^2\\)): the two compensate. A tilted patch is larger by a factor \\(\\ 1/\\cos\\theta\\), and the cosine in the dot product compensates that. So the flux through a patch depends on nothing but the cone it cuts out."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p18"
text = "All directions around a point add up to the solid angle \\(\\ 4\\pi\\), the area of a unit sphere. A closed surface around the charge cuts out every direction exactly once (counted with its sign, when a ray crosses a bumpy surface several times). So the total is \\(\\ \\frac{{Q}}{{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}}\\cdot 4\\pi = \\frac{{Q}}{{\\varepsilon_0}}\\), whatever the shape."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.h19"
text = "STEP 5 — A CHARGE OUTSIDE THE SURFACE"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p20"
text = "Now place the charge outside. A thin cone from it enters the surface through one patch and leaves through another. The solid angle is the same, but the entering patch counts negatively (the field points inward there) and the leaving one positively. They cancel, cone by cone. An outside charge adds nothing to the total flux, although it can change the field at every point of the surface."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.fig21"
text = "The near patch is small, the far one is larger, but both cut the same cone: one contributes −dΦ and the other +dΦ."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.h22"
text = "STEP 6 — MANY CHARGES"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p23"
text = "The field of many charges is the vector sum of their fields (superposition), and the integral of a sum is the sum of integrals. So the total flux is the sum of the fluxes of each charge taken alone: \\(\\ Q_i/\\varepsilon_0\\) for every charge inside and zero for every charge outside."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.fig24"
text = "Only the charges inside count, and with their signs: here q1 and q2. The charge q3 changes the field on the surface but not the total flux."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p25"
text = "For a continuous charge the sum becomes an integral of the volume density \\(\\ \\rho\\):"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.h26"
text = "THE WAY BACK: COULOMB’S LAW FROM GAUSS’S LAW"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p27"
text = "Reverse the logic. Assume Gauss’s law and a point charge. Symmetry says the field is radial and has the same size on a sphere around the charge, so the left side is again \\(\\ E \\cdot 4\\pi r^2\\). Gauss’s law gives \\(\\ Q/\\varepsilon_0\\) on the right. Solve for \\(\\ E\\), then use \\(\\ F = qE\\) for a test charge \\(\\ q\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.note28"
text = "Gauss’s law alone does not give the field in every situation: it fixes only the total flux. The step from the flux to the field needs symmetry. Without it, use superposition or the other equations of electrodynamics."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p29"
text = "For charges at rest the two laws say the same thing. Gauss’s law is the more general one: it is one of Maxwell’s equations and stays true for moving charges, where Coulomb’s formula is no longer exact. Shrink the surface to a point and the flux per unit volume is the divergence of the field:"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.predict30"
text = "A cube is drawn around the same charge instead of a sphere. What is the flux?"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.answer31"
text = "The same, \\(\\ Q/\\varepsilon_0\\). The cone argument does not care about the shape: every direction from the charge is cut out exactly once."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.predict32"
text = "The charge inside a sphere is moved off the centre but stays inside. Does the flux change?"

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.answer33"
text = "No. On the sphere the field is no longer uniform and the angles are no longer zero, but the total flux is still \\(\\ Q/\\varepsilon_0\\): only the charge inside matters."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.p34"
text = "In the program, a ring of field lines around any single charge always has the same number of lines leaving it. Count the lines that leave a small loop around a charge, then around a loop that also contains its neighbour."

[[string]]
id = "help.derivation.try"
text = "Try it: “Dipole” with field lines and flow"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.kicker"
text = "DEEP DIVE"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.title"
text = "Gauss’s law in practice"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.p1"
text = "Gauss’s law is true for every closed surface, but it hands us the field directly only when the symmetry lets us choose a surface on which \\(\\ E\\) has a fixed size and a fixed angle to the surface. Match the surface to the symmetry: a sphere for a point or a ball, a coaxial cylinder for a long line, a flat “pillbox” for a plane."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.note2"
text = "The recipe. 1) From the symmetry, say what the direction of the field is and what it can depend on. 2) Choose a closed surface where \\(\\ E\\) is constant and perpendicular, or parallel, to the surface. 3) Compute the flux: the parts where the field is parallel to the surface give zero. 4) Find \\(\\ Q_{{\\mathrm{{in}}}}\\). 5) Solve for \\(\\ E\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.h3"
text = "A LONG CHARGED LINE"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.p4"
text = "A very long line carries a charge \\(\\ \\lambda\\) per unit length. By symmetry the field points straight away from the line and depends only on the distance \\(\\ r\\). Take a cylinder of radius \\(\\ r\\) and length \\(\\ L\\) around the line. The flux through the side is \\(\\ E\\) times its area \\(\\ 2\\pi r L\\). Through the two caps the flux is zero, because the field runs along them."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.fig5"
text = "A Gaussian cylinder around a long line. The field is perpendicular to the side and parallel to the caps."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.p6"
text = "The length \\(\\ L\\) cancels. The field falls as \\(\\ 1/r\\), not \\(\\ 1/r^2\\): the field lines of a line spread over a circle, not over a sphere."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.h7"
text = "A LARGE FLAT PLATE"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.p8"
text = "A plane carries a charge \\(\\ \\sigma\\) per unit area. The field is perpendicular to the plane and has the same size on both sides at the same distance. Take a “pillbox” that crosses the plane, with two caps of area \\(\\ A\\). The flux goes only through the caps."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.fig9"
text = "A pillbox through a charged plane: the flux goes through the two caps, the sides have none."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.p10"
text = "The result does not depend on the distance: for an ideal infinite plane the lines do not spread, so the field does not weaken. A real plate behaves like this at distances much smaller than its size. Two plates with opposite charges: between them the fields of the two add up to \\(\\ E = \\sigma/\\varepsilon_0\\), outside they cancel. That is the capacitor of topic 9 and the “Capacitor” scene."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.h11"
text = "A CONDUCTOR AT REST"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.p12"
text = "In a conductor at rest the free charges have stopped moving, so the field inside the metal is zero (otherwise they would still be moving). Any Gaussian surface drawn inside the metal then has zero flux, so it encloses zero charge: the excess charge of the conductor can sit only on its surface. Just outside the surface the field is perpendicular to it (a sideways part would push the surface charges along), and it equals \\(\\ \\sigma/\\varepsilon_0\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.fig13"
text = "A conductor at rest: the excess charge on the surface, no field inside, a field perpendicular to the surface outside."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.note14"
text = "These conclusions are for electrostatic equilibrium. The same reasoning explains the Faraday cage: inside a closed metal shell there is no field from charges outside. Rapidly changing fields behave differently."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.q15"
text = "Why does the field of a plane not depend on the distance?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.a16"
text = "The flux through a cap of area \\(\\ A\\) is the same at every height, and the charge inside the pillbox does not change as it grows taller. So \\(\\ E\\) must be the same. A real plate is finite: far away, at distances much larger than its size, it looks like a point charge and the field falls as \\(\\ 1/r^2\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.q17"
text = "Why can’t I use a cylinder for a point charge, or a sphere for a line?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.a18"
text = "The law holds for any surface, but then \\(\\ E\\) is not constant on it and not perpendicular to it, so \\(\\ E\\) cannot be taken out of the integral. The surface helps only when it follows the symmetry."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.predict19"
text = "You move twice as far from a long charged line. How many times weaker is the field?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.answer20"
text = "Two times, because \\(\\ E \\propto 1/r\\). For a point charge it would be four times, because \\(\\ E \\propto 1/r^2\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.predict21"
text = "A hollow metal sphere carries a charge \\(\\ Q\\). What is the field in the empty cavity inside?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.answer22"
text = "Zero. A Gaussian sphere inside the cavity encloses no charge (the charge sits on the outer surface), and by symmetry \\(\\ E\\) is the same everywhere on it, so \\(\\ E = 0\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.p23"
text = "The “Capacitor” scene is two rows of opposite charges, a model of two plates. Between them the field lines run parallel and the probe (F5) reads a nearly constant \\(\\ |E|\\), the \\(\\ \\sigma/\\varepsilon_0\\) of the plate result."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_practice.try"
text = "Try it: “Capacitor” with potential and lines"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.kicker"
text = "DEEP DIVE"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.title"
text = "Gauss’s law: tricky points"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.p1"
text = "This topic collects what is hardest to see the first time: the notation, the step where \\(\\ E\\) leaves the integral, and what flux does and does not tell you."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.fig2"
text = "Left: on a sphere the length of E is the same everywhere, but its direction is not. Right: a patch has an area dA and a normal n; the area vector combines them."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q3"
text = "What are \\(\\ dA\\) and \\(\\ d\\vec{{A}}\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a4"
text = "\\(\\ dA\\) is the area of a tiny patch, a positive number. \\(\\ d\\vec{{A}} = n\\,dA\\) is a vector with that length, perpendicular to the patch. For a closed surface the normal is taken outward."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q5"
text = "Why is \\(\\ \\vec E \\cdot d\\vec A = E\\,dA\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a6"
text = "In general \\(\\ \\vec E \\cdot d\\vec A = E\\,dA\\cos\\theta\\). The simplification holds only where the field is along the outward normal, \\(\\ \\theta = 0^\\circ\\), so \\(\\ \\cos\\theta = 1\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q7"
text = "Why can \\(\\ E\\) be taken out of the integral?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a8"
text = "For a point charge at the centre of a sphere, the distance to every point of the sphere is the same, and by Coulomb’s law the size of the field depends only on distance. So the size \\(\\ E\\) is the same everywhere on the sphere and comes out. The vector \\(\\ \\vec E\\) itself is not the same: its direction changes from point to point."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q9"
text = "Why is \\(\\ \\oint dA = 4\\pi r^2\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a10"
text = "Integrating \\(\\ dA\\) over a surface means adding the areas of all its patches. For a sphere the total area is \\(\\ 4\\pi r^2\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q11"
text = "Why is \\(\\ E = Q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2)\\) not always true?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a12"
text = "It is the field of a point charge, and, outside it, of a spherically symmetric charge. For any other arrangement the field depends on where the charges are, and it must be found by superposition or another method."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q13"
text = "Why does Gauss’s law work when the field is not uniform?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a14"
text = "It is about the total flux through the whole closed surface, not the field at one point. The flux can differ from patch to patch, but its sum is fixed by the charge inside."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q15"
text = "Where does the sum of charges in Gauss’s law come from?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a16"
text = "From superposition: the field of several charges is the sum of their fields, and by the linearity of the integral the total flux is the sum of the individual fluxes. Charges inside give \\(\\ Q_i/\\varepsilon_0\\), charges outside give zero."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q17"
text = "How are Coulomb’s law and Gauss’s law related?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a18"
text = "Coulomb’s law gives the field of a point charge. From it we get the flux through a sphere, and with superposition the whole integral law. Backwards, Gauss’s law with spherical symmetry gives the field of a point charge."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.q19"
text = "What does flux mean physically?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.a20"
text = "It is not a flow of matter. It is a mathematical measure of how much field passes through a surface, built from the size of the field, the area and the angle between the field and the normal."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.leg21"
text = "\\(\\ E\\) is the size, \\(\\ \\vec E\\) is the vector with a direction."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.leg22"
text = "a small area is a number; the area vector also has a direction, along the normal."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.leg23"
text = "the dot product: \\(\\ E\\,dA\\cos\\theta\\), a number, not a vector."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.leg24"
text = "a zero total flux means zero total charge inside, not zero field."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.leg25"
text = "the whole area of the closed surface; for a sphere \\(\\ 4\\pi r^2\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.leg26"
text = "only charges inside, with signs: positive and negative can cancel; outside charges do not count in the flux, but change the field."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss_pitfalls.p27"
text = "Pull the factor \\(\\ E\\) out of the integral only when two conditions hold on the surface: the size of \\(\\ E\\) is constant, and the field is along the normal (or perpendicular to it) everywhere. If either fails, keep the integral. Gauss’s law fixes the flux, not the field at every point; to get the field from it you need symmetry."

