[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.kicker"
text = "HISTORY"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.title"
text = "Coulomb’s law: how it was found"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.p1"
text = "For about twenty-five centuries people knew that rubbed amber attracts light things, and for most of that time nobody asked by how much. A law of electricity needs numbers: how does the force depend on the charges and on the distance? The answer took almost two centuries and the work of several people, one of whom never published it."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl2"
text = "By a later tradition, Thales of Miletus notices that rubbed amber attracts light objects. The Greek word for amber, ēlektron, gives us “electricity”."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.yr3"
text = "~600 BC"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl4"
text = "William Gilbert, in De Magnete, separates electric attraction from magnetic and shows that many substances, not only amber, can be electrified by rubbing."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl5"
text = "Charles du Fay finds two kinds of electrification, from glass and from resin: bodies of the same kind repel, of different kinds attract."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl6"
text = "Benjamin Franklin describes electricity as one “fluid” that a body has in excess or in deficit. He introduces the signs plus and minus and the idea that charge is conserved. By chance his choice made the electron negative."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl7"
text = "Franklin sees that a charged metal cup does not pull a cork ball hanging inside it. Joseph Priestley repeats the experiment and notes that Newton had proved the same for gravity inside a hollow shell, which is true only for a force that falls as 1/r². He suggests that the electric force falls the same way."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl8"
text = "John Robison in Edinburgh measures the repulsion of two charged balls directly and finds that the force falls with an exponent close to 2.06."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl9"
text = "Henry Cavendish tests the 1/r² law with a sphere inside a sphere and finds that the exponent is 2 within two per cent. He publishes nothing; Maxwell finds his notes and prints them in 1879."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl10"
text = "Charles-Augustin de Coulomb builds a torsion balance, measures the force between small charged balls and publishes the law in the memoirs of the Paris Academy. The force varies as 1/r²; and, since he can halve a charge by touching the ball with an identical uncharged one, it is proportional to the product of the charges."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl11"
text = "An international congress of electricians names the unit of charge the coulomb."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl12"
text = "Alpha particles of Ernest Rutherford scatter off gold nuclei just as Coulomb’s law predicts, down to distances of about 10⁻¹⁴ m, a trillion times smaller than Coulomb could reach."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl13"
text = "Plimpton and Lawton repeat Cavendish’s idea with a modern electrometer: the exponent is 2 to about two parts in a billion."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.tl14"
text = "Williams, Faller and Hill push the same test further: any deviation is smaller than one part in 10¹⁵."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.h15"
text = "HOW COULOMB DID IT"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.p16"
text = "A thin silver wire holds a light horizontal rod with a small ball a at one end and a counterweight at the other. A second ball b is fixed nearby. When both are charged they repel, and the rod turns and twists the wire. The twist of a thin wire is proportional to the angle, so the angle measures the force. Turning the top of the wire brings the balls back closer, and from the extra twist needed Coulomb found how the force depends on the distance: when the distance was halved, the twist needed to hold the balls rose about fourfold."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.fig17"
text = "Coulomb’s torsion balance. Top view (left): the rod turns by the angle θ because b pushes a. Side view (right): the wire, the rod, the balls and the fixed ball b let in through the cover."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.p18"
text = "Two limits make the experiment hard. Charge leaks away through the air within minutes, so every reading had to be quick. And the force of attraction cannot be measured on a balance that is free to turn: the ball would snap onto its partner. For attraction Coulomb used a different method, timing how long a charged ball swings around a second, oppositely charged one, and he found the same law."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.h19"
text = "CAVENDISH’S NULL TEST"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.p20"
text = "Cavendish chose a cleverer test: instead of measuring a force, look for a charge that must be zero. If the force falls exactly as \\(\\ 1/r^2\\), charge on a conductor sits only on its outer surface and the field inside a closed shell is zero (topic 6). He put a sphere inside a hollow one, joined them with a wire, charged the whole thing, took the wire away and tested the inner sphere with a sensitive electrometer. It carried no charge. With any other exponent a trace of charge would have remained on the inner sphere. Modern versions of this test are many orders of magnitude more sensitive than Cavendish’s."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.fig21"
text = "Cavendish’s sphere in a sphere. Left: both are charged through a wire. Right: after the wire is removed, the inner sphere holds no charge, which is only possible if the exponent is exactly 2."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.q22"
text = "Is Coulomb’s law exact?"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.a23"
text = "Experiments find no deviation from \\(\\ 1/r^2\\) at the level of one part in \\(\\ 10^{{15}}\\) over laboratory distances. A deviation would mean that the photon has a mass. At extremely small distances quantum electrodynamics predicts small corrections to the law, because the vacuum around a charge is polarised."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.q24"
text = "Why did Franklin’s plus and minus stick?"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.a25"
text = "They are a convention, and only the pair matters: like repel, unlike attract. Franklin did not know which charge carries the moving particles. After the discovery of the electron it turned out to be the negative one, which is why the current in a wire is said to flow opposite to the motion of the electrons."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.p26"
text = "The “Rutherford” scene is the 1911 experiment in miniature: a fast positive charge flies at a heavy positive one, and its path bends exactly as the \\(\\ 1/r^2\\) repulsion says. Turn on the trails (F6) to see the curve."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_electric.try"
text = "Try it: “Rutherford” with trails"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.kicker"
text = "HISTORY"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.title"
text = "From force to field: Faraday, Gauss, Maxwell"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.p1"
text = "Coulomb described how one charge acts on another across empty space. The idea that space itself carries something, a field, took another century. This topic follows how Gauss’s law and its neighbours grew out of that idea, and why the field turned out to be more than a convenient picture."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.fig2"
text = "Two ways to think about the same force. Left: action at a distance, each charge acts on the other directly. Right: the field, the charge changes the space around it, and the other charge responds to the field where it sits."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl3"
text = "Siméon-Denis Poisson applies the potential, invented for gravity by Lagrange and Laplace, to electric charges on conductors. The potential becomes the main tool of electrostatics."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl4"
text = "Carl Friedrich Gauss, in a paper on the attraction of ellipsoids, proves special cases of the theorem that relates the flux through a closed surface to what is inside it. In 1839 he states it for any force that falls as 1/r²: the flux depends only on the “mass”, or charge, inside."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl5"
text = "Mikhail Ostrogradsky, born in the Poltava region, proves the theorem in its general form, for any closed surface. In mathematics it is now called the Gauss–Ostrogradsky theorem."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl6"
text = "George Green, a self-taught miller from Nottingham, prints a small book that coins the word “potential” and proves a close relative of the same theorem. It sells to a few dozen subscribers and is nearly forgotten until Kelvin finds it in 1845."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl7"
text = "Michael Faraday discovers electromagnetic induction. A changing magnetic field produces an electric field: charges are not the only source."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl8"
text = "Faraday, with almost no mathematics, pictures space filled with lines of force running from charges, magnets and currents, and refuses to believe in action at a distance. Maxwell later wrote that where mathematicians saw centres of force acting from afar, Faraday saw lines of force filling all space."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl9"
text = "James Clerk Maxwell, in “On Faraday’s Lines of Force”, turns the lines into mathematics, using the flow of an imagined fluid as a model. The number of lines through a surface becomes what we call flux."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl10"
text = "Maxwell adds the displacement current: a changing electric field acts like a current. The equations now have wave solutions with a speed close to the speed of light, which Weber and Kohlrausch had measured from electrical units in 1856. He concludes that light is an electromagnetic wave."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl11"
text = "Maxwell’s Treatise on Electricity and Magnetism collects the theory in about twenty equations written out in components. Gauss’s law is one of them."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl12"
text = "Oliver Heaviside, and in the same years Josiah Willard Gibbs, rewrite the theory in vector notation as the four equations we use today. Gauss’s law gets its compact form ∇·E = ρ/ε₀."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl13"
text = "Heinrich Hertz generates and detects radio waves in the laboratory and measures their speed. The field is a real thing: it carries energy through empty space."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.tl14"
text = "Feynman, Schwinger and Tomonaga complete quantum electrodynamics. In it Coulomb’s law is the classical limit of the exchange of photons between charges."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.h15"
text = "WHAT CHANGED"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.p16"
text = "With Coulomb the question was: how strongly do two charges act on each other? With Faraday and Maxwell it became: what is the field at this point, and how does it spread? Gauss’s law belongs to the second way of thinking. It says nothing about forces between pairs of charges. It states what the field must do around any charge, whatever the shape of the surface. That is why it survives when charges move, while the simple form of Coulomb’s law does not."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.q17"
text = "Who discovered Gauss’s law?"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.a18"
text = "No one at a single moment. Gauss proved the mathematical theorem for inverse-square forces in 1813 and 1839, Ostrogradsky and Green proved the general theorem, and Maxwell put it into the electromagnetic theory as one of its equations. The name was fixed later, and Heaviside gave it its present form."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.q19"
text = "Why does the field of a charge “spread” at a finite speed?"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.a20"
text = "Because it is a real physical thing and not a bookkeeping device. If a charge is moved, the field far away learns about it only after the time the distance divided by the speed of light. This is what the program leaves out: it recomputes the field everywhere at once (see the last topic)."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.p21"
text = "The field lines and the flow in the program are Faraday’s picture, and the number of lines from each charge (topic 5) is Gauss’s law at work. The program computes the field by Coulomb’s law and superposition, which is correct for charges at rest or moving slowly."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_field.try"
text = "Try it: “Dipole” with lines, flow and potential"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.kicker"
text = "HISTORY"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.title"
text = "Magnetism and moving charges: from Ørsted to Lorentz"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.p1"
text = "For centuries magnetism and electricity were two separate subjects: lodestones and compasses on one side, amber and sparks on the other. In 1820 a lecture demonstration showed that they are connected, and within weeks the connection was being measured. A few more decades gave the force on a moving charge that the program uses."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.fig2"
text = "Ørsted’s experiment, top view. With no current the needle points along the wire (faint). With the current on, it turns toward the field of the wire, which is across the wire, and settles at the angle α between the two fields."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl3"
text = "William Gilbert shows that the Earth itself is a giant magnet, which explains the compass, and separates magnetism from electricity."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl4"
text = "Hans Christian Ørsted notices that a compass needle near a wire with a current turns and settles across the wire, not along it. It is the first sign that a current makes a magnetic field. He reports it in July and the news crosses Europe in weeks."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl5"
text = "André-Marie Ampère shows that two parallel wires attract when the currents run the same way and repel when they run opposite, and that a coil acts like a magnet. Jean-Baptiste Biot and Félix Savart measure the field of a long wire: it falls as 1/r and runs in circles around the wire. Laplace casts the result as a law for each small piece of current, the Biot–Savart law."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl6"
text = "Ampère publishes his mathematical theory of the forces between currents."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl7"
text = "Michael Faraday, and independently Joseph Henry in America, find the opposite effect: a changing magnetic field makes a current in a wire. The dynamo and the transformer follow."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl8"
text = "Edwin Hall finds that a magnetic field pushes the moving charges in a flat conductor to one side and produces a voltage across it. It is the first direct sign that the moving charges themselves feel the magnetic force."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl9"
text = "J. J. Thomson (1881), Oliver Heaviside (1889) and Hendrik Lorentz (1892) work out the force on a moving point charge. It is now called the Lorentz force, \\(\\ F = q(E + v \\times B)\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl10"
text = "J. J. Thomson sends a beam of cathode rays through crossed electric and magnetic fields. When the two deflections cancel, the speed is E/B, the drift of topic 18. From the bending of the beam he finds the ratio of the charge to the mass. The electron is discovered."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl11"
text = "Albert Einstein’s special relativity shows that the magnetic force is the electric force seen from a moving frame. That is why in the program the magnetic constant is tied to the speed of light: \\(\\ \\mu_0/4\\pi = k/c^2\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.tl12"
text = "Ernest Lawrence, with Stanley Livingston, builds the cyclotron: ions circle in a magnetic field, and the time of one turn does not depend on the speed (topic 17), so a fixed-frequency electric field can keep pushing them."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.q13"
text = "Why does the needle turn across the wire and not toward it?"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.a14"
text = "Because the field of a wire circles it, so at the needle it points across the wire. The magnetic force is not a force along the line between the bodies like Coulomb’s. That surprised physicists of the time, who expected an attraction or repulsion between the wire and the poles of the needle."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.q15"
text = "Is a magnet different from a current?"

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.a16"
text = "Not in what matters here. Ampère proposed that a magnet is full of tiny current loops, and in the atoms these are the motions of the electrons and their spins. A coil with a current and a bar magnet make the same kind of field."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.p17"
text = "The “Coil” scene is a current loop: its field is built from the law of Biot and Savart, and charges drift around it. The “Cyclotron” scene is Lawrence’s idea in the simplest form: a charge circling in a uniform field."

[[string]]
id = "help.history_magnetic.try"
text = "Try it: “Coil” with trails and the probe"

[[string]]
id = "help.h.timeline"
text = "TIMELINE"

[[string]]
id = "help.fig.action_distance"
text = "action at a distance"

[[string]]
id = "help.fig.through_field"
text = "through the field"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.fig1"
text = "Like charges push apart, unlike charges pull together. F1 acts on charge 1 and F2 on charge 2: they are equal in size and opposite in direction (Newton’s third law)."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.fig2"
text = "Twice as far, and the same influence is spread over four times the area, so the force is four times weaker. Three times as far, nine times weaker."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.fig1"
text = "A charge creates a field around it: an arrow at every point shows the force on a positive test charge. The arrows get shorter as 1/r². A test charge q at some point feels F = qE."

[[string]]
id = "help.superposition.fig1"
text = "At the point P each charge makes its own field, E1 and E2. The total field E is their vector sum, found by the parallelogram rule."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.fig1"
text = "Lines leave positive charges and arrive at negative ones. Two lines cannot cross: at the crossing the field would have two directions at once."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.fig1"
text = "Three closed surfaces. A charge inside: all the lines leave, the flux is positive. A charge outside: the lines enter and leave, the net flux is zero. A dipole inside: every line that leaves comes back, the net flux is zero."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.see"
text = "The next three topics go deeper: where Gauss’s law comes from, how to use it for a line, a plane and a conductor, and the points that are hardest to see the first time."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.fig1"
text = "Equipotentials of a point charge for equal steps of φ. They crowd near the charge, where φ changes fastest, and the field E, perpendicular to them, is stronger there."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.fig1"
text = "Between the plates the field is uniform and the potential falls steadily: the equipotentials are equally spaced lines, and the voltage between the plates is the field times the distance d."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.fig1"
text = "The field is the slope of the potential with a minus sign. Near the charge the curve is steep and the field is strong; far away the slope is gentle and the field is weak."

[[string]]
id = "help.pair_energy.fig1"
text = "The energy of a pair against the distance. Like charges: U is positive and grows as they approach, so two such charges flying at each other stop at r min, where all the kinetic energy has turned into U. Unlike charges: U is negative, a bound pair has less energy than a separated one."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.fig1"
text = "The same curve, with the zero of energy chosen in two ways. The numbers on the scales differ, but the difference ΔU between two points is the same: only differences have physical meaning."

[[string]]
id = "help.bfield.fig1"
text = "Left: a current coming toward you, B circles it counter-clockwise. Right: the right-hand rule, the thumb along the current and the fingers along B."

[[string]]
id = "help.lorentz.fig1"
text = "The magnetic force is perpendicular to both v and B. Here B goes into the page: a positive charge is pushed to the left of its motion, a negative one to the right."

[[string]]
id = "help.cyclotron.fig1"
text = "In a uniform B a charge moves in a circle: the magnetic force is always toward the centre and does no work, so the speed does not change."

[[string]]
id = "help.exb.fig1"
text = "E points down and B goes into the page. The charge speeds up along E, the magnetic force bends it, and the loops add up to a steady drift E/B to the right, perpendicular to both fields."

[[string]]
id = "help.coil.fig1"
text = "A current loop seen from the side: the field goes up through the loop, comes back around outside, and is strongest inside the loop."

